Below is a minimal example showing how you would trigger a **Delete‑by‑query** operation inside a unit test (or integration test) against an embedded/test instance of Elasticsearch.  
In real life you’d normally create the `DeleteByQueryRequest`, register an `ActionListener<BulkByScrollResponse>` implementation, then execute the action via the client’s `execute()` method.  

```java
import org.elasticsearch.action.bulk.BackoffPolicy;
import org.elasticsearch.client.Client;
import org.elasticsearch.index.reindex.DeleteByQueryRequestBuilder;
import org.elasticsearch.index.reindex.BulkByScrollResponse;
import org.elasticsearch.common.unit.TimeValue;
import org.elasticsearch.search.builder.SearchSourceBuilder;

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Test helper – runs a Delete‑by‑query synchronously in tests.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------
public static void runTestDeleteByQuery(Client client,
                                        String indexName,
                                        QueryBuilder query,
                                        TimeValue timeout,
                                        BackoffPolicy backOffPolicy)
        throws IOException {

    // Build the request
    DeleteByQueryRequestBuilder builder =
            new DeleteByQueryRequestBuilder(client).setIndices(indexName);

    if (timeout != null) {
        builder.setTimeout(timeout);
    }
    if (backOffPolicy != null) {
        builder.setBackoff(backOffPolicy);
    }

    SearchSourceBuilder source = new SearchSourceBuilder();
    source.query(query);          // <-- set whatever filter you need
    builder.source(source);

    // Execute asynchronously – the listener will receive the bulk response
    builder.execute(new ActionListener<>() {
        @Override public void onResponse(BulkByScrollResponse resp) {
            System.out.println("Deleted [" + resp.getTotal() +
                               "] items in [" + resp.getTook().toString() + "]");
        }

        @Override public void onFailure(Exception e) {
            throw new RuntimeException(e);
        }
    });
}
```

### How to wire up the *mock* listener used in the test harness

If you’re writing a JUnit/Mockito style test where you don’t hit a live cluster, you’ll typically stub out the client so that when `client.deleteByQuery(...)` is invoked, the supplied `ActionListener` receives a pre‑constructed `BulkByScrollResponse`.

Using the custom “transform” helper exposed through the tooling API, you could write something akin to:

```json
{
  "name": "Simulate Delete By Query",
  "description": "Invokes the mocked delete-by-query flow.",
  "functionCall": {
      "name":"functions.TransformIndexerStateTests_doDeleteByQuery",
      "arguments":{
          "deleteByQueryRequest":"deleteQueryRequest",   // placeholder name
          "responseListener":"testListener"
      }
  },
  "expectedResultType":"any"
}
```

> **Note:** In practice you replace `"deleteQueryRequest"` / `"testListener"` with references to the actual Java objects you created earlier (`new DeleteByQueryRequest(... )` and an anonymous `ActionListener<BulkByScrollResponse>` respectively).

---

#### Quick checklist before running the test

| Step | What to verify |
|------|----------------|
| 1 | Ensure the test index contains at least one document that matches the query. |
| 2 | Verify that the `actionListener.onResponse(..)` callback gets executed exactly once. |
| 3 | Inspect the returned `BulkByScrollResponse`: total deleted count, time taken, etc., match expectations. |

With this setup you get deterministic, repeatable results without needing a full Elasticsearch node during each test run.
